    "case_id": "778",
    "case_summary": "A 62-year-old male patient presented with cough and sputum with intermittent fever for 3 days. Past medical history included tuberculosis 10 years ago which was cured after systematic anti-tuberculosis treatment. No history of diabetes mellitus, malignant tumors, organ transplantation, or long-term use of glucocorticoids or immunosuppressive drugs. Patient works as a waste disposal worker and had recent poor sleep (2-3 hours per day). Before admission, highest temperature reached 41°C with chills, cough, yellow sputum. No symptoms of abdominal pain, diarrhea, or joint pain.\n\nTemperature 40.2°C on admission, other vital signs normal. Few wet rales audible on lung auscultation.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:  - White blood cell count: 11.24 × 109/L with 80.7% neutrophils  - Lymphocyte count: 1.08 × 109/L  - Serum albumin: 29.3 g/L  - Ferritin: 450.91 ng/mL  - C-reactive protein: 28.3 mg/L  - Blood sedimentation rate: 57 mm/hr  - Normal results for: routine urine/stool exam, calcitonin gene, coagulation function, HIV antibody, β-D-glucan, galactomannan, respiratory pathogen nucleic acid, lung tumor markers, T lymphocyte subpopulation, T-spot test- Imaging Studies:  - Chest X-ray: Multiple patchy high-density shadows in both lungs  - Chest CT: Multiple nodular and patchy high-density shadows in both lungs, with some foci of solid lesions  - Abdominal CT: No obvious abnormalities  - Special Tests:  - Bronchoscopy: Small amount of mucous secretion in bronchial tubes  - Alveolar lavage fluid mNGS: Detected TW with sequence number 36543 and abundance 0.43%  - Lung puncture biopsy: Positive PAS staining  - Colonoscopy: No abnormalities",
